#: src/types-and-values/exercise.md:20
msgid "// The base case.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/exercise.md:21 src/types-and-values/exercise.md:24
msgid "\"Implement this\""
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/exercise.md:23
msgid "// The recursive case.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/exercise.md:30
msgid "\"fib({n}) = {}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/exercise.md:37
msgid "This exercise is a classic introduction to recursion."
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/exercise.md:38
msgid ""
"Encourage students to think about the base cases and the recursive step."
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/exercise.md:39
msgid ""
"The question \"When will this function panic?\" is a hint to think about "
"integer overflow. The Fibonacci sequence grows quickly!"
msgstr ""

#: src/types-and-values/exercise.md:41
msgid ""
"Students might come up with an iterative solution as well, which is a great "
"opportunity to discuss the trade-offs between recursion and iteration (e.g., "
"performance, stack overflow for deep recursion)."
msgstr ""
